    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d653">

                <pb facs="0695" n="591" sameAs="frus:frus1932v05/pg591"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">816.01/138: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the Chargé in El Salvador</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">McCafferty</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1932-03-31T18:00:00-05:00">March 31, 1932—6
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>41. Your 68, March 18, 5 p.m., and 70, March 22, 1 p.m.<anchor n="20"
                        xml:id="d653-f1"/> The press on March 30 published an AP despatch from San
                    Salvador, dated March 29, to the effect that a petition signed by more than
                    20,000 Salvadorans was presented to Martínez urging him to maintain himself in power as long as the
                    present difficult economic situation exists. Is this report substantially true
                    and, if so, what effect do you think it will have on the plans of the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">de facto</hi> authorities? What, if anything, is being
                    done by these authorities, now that Holy Week has passed, to carry out their
                    indicated intention to reorganize the Government on a basis where recognition
                    can be extended to it?</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Castle</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
